* Tue Oct 06 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package, and it settles the decision docs/45 left open: the avahi
  reflector arrives as an idempotent in-place merge of two keys in
  /etc/avahi/avahi-daemon.conf, not as a copy of that file. Shipping the whole
  file would clobber every future Fedora change to it, and avahi has no conf.d
  directory to drop into instead.
- The firewalld half stops being a file edit at all. The source-port rule goes
  in through firewall-cmd, which is additive, queryable and removable by name,
  so firewalld's zone file stays firewalld's and this package owns no part of
  it. A packaged firewalld service definition was considered and rejected: a
  service applies to both address families, and the rule docs/45 measured the
  exposure of is family="ipv4" only.
- Nothing is written to avahi's config until the daemon binary has been grepped
  for every key about to be set. avahi refuses to START on an unknown key, so
  writing reflect-filters onto a host whose avahi predates it would leave that
  host with no mDNS at all rather than an unfiltered reflector. The version
  string cannot answer this -- Fedora's 0.9~rc2 reports 0.8 and the feature
  works -- so the binary is asked instead, and the whole change is refused
  rather than degraded.
- If the daemon will not come back after the edit, the edit is rolled back and
  avahi is restarted on the configuration it had a second earlier. Nothing else
  in this project edits a host daemon's own config file.
- %%check is strict and is the reason to believe any of the above: the stock
  Fedora avahi-daemon.conf rendered through the merge must equal, byte for
  byte, the file that was on the host when a test page came out of the Canon on
  2026-09-11. Nine further cases cover idempotence, the absent-section and
  duplicate-key paths, both refusals, the capability gate in both directions,
  and the rule the tool builds against the captured zone file.
- Said plainly rather than glossed: an uninstall does not undo the edit, and
  afterwards `rpm -V avahi` reports avahi-daemon.conf as modified, because it
  is. The copy taken before the first edit is kept beside it and
  `waydroid-mdns-reflector --revert` restores it and removes the firewall rule.
  %%preun names that command while the tool that runs it is still installed.
- The %%post uses --no-restart and the tool says on stderr that avahi is still
  running the old configuration. A package here names a restart rather than
  restarting somebody else's daemon inside an rpm transaction -- the same
  choice waydroid-ext-overlay-sync makes about the container. The firewall half
  applies live either way, and nothing ever calls `firewall-cmd --reload`.
- The boot unit is WantedBy=multi-user.target and After= avahi and firewalld,
  not the ExecStartPre docs/47 prescribed for the non-overlay reconcilers. mDNS
  is discovery over a live socket -- the Canon appeared in a session that was
  already running -- so there is nothing for waydroid-container.service to be
  ordered against, and inventing that dependency would buy nothing.
- The filter list is %%config(noreplace) at /etc/waydroid-mdns.conf, flat, with
  no directory of ours around it. waydroid-ext-dexopt's values are a %%config
  under /usr/share and fail packaging/test-install.sh for it: the .rpmsave rpm
  leaves behind keeps the directory above from being removed on erase.
